{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779119582,"nonce":"+l8ypwXVQvG8BT0YzqHAnxbVk/Y=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCndB3w","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/128","origin":"github"},"title":"Investigate op-router forwarding asymmetry; re-evaluate 04-14 masquerade-removal decision","message":"## Context\n\nThe 04-27 deployment ended with a partial operator-side network failure\n(see [`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md)\n§ \"Op-router forwarding asymmetry\", § \"Recovery: many cycles, root cause indeterminate\"):\n\n- Established TCP/SSH sessions stayed alive all day.\n- **New** connections from operator (deadpool, 192.168.13.x) to mercat / gabby failed.\n- Op-router-sourced ping (192.168.13.1 → mercat) **worked**.\n- Deadpool-sourced ping (192.168.13.x → mercat) **failed**, with ICMP arriving at\n  mercat per `tcpdump` on `br-lan` but no reply visible.\n- Recovery required many power cycles across op router + MikroTik bridge + boat.\n  Root cause not isolated.\n\nThat asymmetry — same destination, different sources, different result — is\nexactly what NAT (specifically the operator-router LAN-zone masquerade we\ndisabled on 2026-04-14) would have collapsed.\n\n## 04-14 masquerade-removal context\n\n`ccomjhc_project11/documentation/bizzyboat_network_debug_2026-04-14.md` disabled\n`masq` on the op-router LAN zone and added static return routes on the bridge\ndevices, deliberately, to surface real source IPs in bridge-device logs. The\ndecision review (`docs/bizzyboat_deployment_log.md:1642-1666`) noted \"no known\nunintended side effects\" — written before the 04-27 event.\n\n## Subsequent observations\n\n- **2026-04-29** ([`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-29_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-29_dev_logs.md)):\n  `ping.bizzy: salmon_direct` flapped 25–70% loss over the WiFi-bridge path\n  while VPN-routed equivalents were 1–5% bad. Candidate causes include\n  op-router NAT path / boat-router masquerade rules; not yet root-caused.\n- **2026-05-01**: bandwidth-saturation problems were the dominant pattern;\n  unrelated to NAT/forwarding plumbing.\n\n## Working hypothesis\n\nRemoving masquerade may have left a return-path / conntrack / zone-classification\ngap that surfaces under sustained load. Re-enabling masquerade would mask the\nsymptom but not the underlying state pathology.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **Snapshot first.** Capture config + state from all 4 network devices\n   (boat Teltonika, op Teltonika, OmniTIK, SXTsq). Tracked as a sub-task:\n   `CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#\u003cTBD\u003e` (snapshots go to that repo because it\n   contains the private network config).\n2. **Synthesize** against the documented architecture\n   ([`docs/bizzyboat_network.md`](docs/bizzyboat_network.md)) and the 04-14\n   change record. Specifically check:\n   - Op-router LAN-zone masq still `0`, any accreted custom rules,\n     FORWARD-chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24`.\n   - Boat-router FORWARD-chain handling of `192.168.13.0/24`.\n   - Bridge-device return routes still present and persistent post power-cycle.\n   - Conntrack ceilings vs counts under steady state.\n   - Any drift from the documented 04-14 state.\n3. **Decide** based on data:\n   - Fix the underlying forwarding-asymmetry source if identified, OR\n   - Re-enable masquerade as a pragmatic class-prep choice, accepting the\n     diagnostic-IP cost knowingly.\n\n## Related\n\n- Sub-task: `CCOMJHC/ccomjhc_project11#\u003cTBD\u003e` (snapshot collection)\n- Related (not a duplicate): #97 (op-side `/diagnostics` recorder)\n- Source incident: [`docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md`](docs/logs/2026/2026-04-27_dev_logs.md)\n- 04-14 change: `ccomjhc_project11/documentation/bizzyboat_network_debug_2026-04-14.md`\n\n---\n\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}